nside `'…'` are literal, and an unclosed quote denies the call; the command, flag and operand test applies to each token after quote removal, so a quoted flag counts as a flag (`ls '-S'` is `ls -S` and `grep ''--filt=x y f` is `grep --filt=x y f`, both denied), and in `cat "'" $(touch p) "'"` the `$(` stands outside quotes and denies the call (bash runs that substitution, orchestrator check of 2026-11-06, `reports/concept-v0527-rv.md:11`). Outside quotes the call holds no `$` and no backtick (no parameter, arithmetic or command expansion), no backslash, no `{` or `}`, no glob character (`*`, `?`, `[`, `]`), no redirect or subshell character (`<`, `>`, `(`, `)`) and no `#` (bash and PowerShell read it as the start of a comment, which ends at a newline after which the next line runs, while the lexer would read a quote after it as open; v0.5.29, `review-verify` run 90 🔴-1, executed: bash ran `touch` hidden behind `ls #'⏎touch p⏎#'`); anywhere in the call, quoted or not, every character is printable ASCII, a tab or a newline, else the call is denied (PowerShell closes a single-quoted string at the typographic quotes U+2018 and U+2019, which the lexer reads as literal; v0.5.29, run 90 🟡-1, executed on 2026-11-06: Windows PowerShell 5.1 printed the output of a `$(…)` placed between `'a’` and `‘b'`); segments are joined only by `|`, `;`, `&&`, `||` or a newline, each a named command, and a single `&` is denied. Why an allowlist of flags replaces the denylist: the denylist checked the text piece by piece while bash forms the words later, and three expansions handed a command a flag or a command the check never saw: `$'…'` quoting (`git diff $'--output=f'` wrote `f`, `review-verify` run 81 🔴-1), unquoted parameter expansion (`date -u +%s$IFS-d$IFS@0` ran `date -d`, `review-verify` run 83 🟡-1; `lib/shell-allowlist.mjs:4`, `:30`, `:36` of the SL-2c build) and arithmetic expansion (`cat 'a[$(touch p)]' ; cat $[_]` ran the hidden `touch`, `adversarial-auditor` run 84 F1), each executed against the build; an abbreviated long option (`--filt…`) passes a prefix list as well (run 84 NV-2). With no expansion and only named literal flags, bash has no step left at which an unchecked flag or command appears, and `wc -c`, `head -n 5` and `grep -c` pass again (the `-c` over-deny of run 83 🟢-4 goes away). `git` stays out, because a repository configuration (`diff.<driver>.textconv`, `core.fsmonitor`) can run a program on an otherwise read-only call (run 81 🟢-3), and `rg` because `rg --pre` runs a program (run 79 🟢-E). Operands are not checked against secret paths (`cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa` passes the allowlist); the first layer for them is the sandbox `denyRead` of §5.6 item 5 (`adversarial-auditor` run 84 F7). `Skill` stays open: a skill call writes nothing itself, and its `!` command lines run through the Bash or PowerShell tool "the same way it runs Claude's own shell commands" (skills.md:751-759, fetched 2026-11-06), so the allowlist applies to them when the PreToolUse hooks see them, which probe A-24 records (§11.1; `adversarial-auditor` run 84 NV-3; Q-26). Every guard takes the stop from one lookup, `stopFor(input, env)` of `lib/stop-guard.mjs`, which returns a deny reason for an unreadable run pointer (`<data>/active-runs.json`), an unreadable `run.json`, a missing, unreadable or malformed `counters.json` of the active run and an active stop, so the guards fail closed alike (run 81 🟡-1; `review-code` run 85 🟡-1 and 🟡-3: the SL-2c build copies the lookup into each guard, `guard-shell.mjs` fails open on a lookup error, `hooks/guard-shell.mjs:62-67`, and a missing `counters.json` counts as no stop, `lib/stop-guard.mjs:11`; `adversarial-auditor` run 84 F8; AC-12.37). The deny reason names the agent id, its role and task (for a stop entry of an unbound agent: no bound spawn, so no task), the cause ("finished without a captured verdict: no `SubagentHandback` call reached `check-handback.mjs`, or its handback was refused") and the owner's way out: end the 